<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New paroxysmal atrial fibrillation in a fit 66-year-old, with thyroid tests showing hyperthyroidism and a positive TSH receptor antibody. Please confirm Graves&apos; disease, assess the eyes, and advise on definitive treatment and on the thyroid course afterwards. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree this is Graves&apos; disease, or is an uptake scan needed to exclude a nodular goiter or thyroiditis?</item><item>Which definitive treatment do you advise (radioactive iodine, long-term methimazole, or surgery), given his age and atrial fibrillation?</item><item>How should methimazole and the beta-blocker be handled before and after treatment?</item><item>Is there any Graves&apos; orbitopathy, and does he need steroid cover?</item><item>What thyroid course should we expect afterwards, and when should levothyroxine start?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Bush is seen at the request of Dr. Whitford, twelve days after starting methimazole 20 mg daily. Before the atrial fibrillation he had noticed more tiredness on his runs, and some weight loss despite a good appetite. He feels better on methimazole and metoprolol. He has had no neck pain, recent viral illness, iodine contrast, or amiodarone, and no eye pain, grittiness, bulging, or double vision. He has had no methimazole side effects. He has never used tobacco. He wants a treatment that settles the problem quickly so he can get back to running and a full schedule. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>George Herbert Walker Bush was born in Milton, Massachusetts, on June 12, 1924. [D]</item><item>On May 4, 1991, he developed atrial fibrillation while running at Camp David; it presented as unusual fatigue during the run. He was flown to Bethesda Naval Hospital, where an electrocardiogram showed no abnormality except the irregular rhythm and a cardiac ultrasound showed no structural abnormality and normal heart function. Treatment began with digoxin. [D]</item><item>On May 5, 1991, the White House said he still had atrial fibrillation, with no sign of heart damage or heart attack, and that procainamide had been added to digoxin; electrical cardioversion would be considered if the rhythm did not return to normal. [D]</item><item>His normal rhythm returned late on May 5, atrial fibrillation recurred early on May 6, and cardioversion was judged unnecessary because his response to medication was encouraging. He was discharged on May 6 and was in normal sinus rhythm later that day, on digoxin and procainamide. [D]</item><item>Later in the same week he was diagnosed with Graves&apos; disease. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

