<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Graves&apos; disease with hyperthyroidism (E05.00). Confirmed: suppressed TSH with raised free T4 and total T3, a positive TSH receptor antibody, and a diffuse goiter. No uptake scan is needed. Responding to methimazole: free T4 2.2 ng/dL, total T3 176 ng/dL. Neutrophils and ALT are normal. [S]</item><item>Graves&apos; orbitopathy. None (clinical activity score 0). As a never smoker without eye disease, his risk of new or worse eye disease after radioactive iodine is low, and steroid cover is not needed. [S]</item><item>Atrial fibrillation triggered by thyrotoxicosis (I48.0). In sinus rhythm on metoprolol. Most thyrotoxic atrial fibrillation does not recur once thyroid levels are controlled, which favors prompt definitive treatment. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The hospital ECG and echocardiogram, the intake thyroid tests and TSH receptor antibody, the baseline blood count and liver and kidney tests, and today&apos;s free T4, total T3, TSH, neutrophil count, and ALT. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Radioactive iodine as definitive treatment: it is effective, quick, and avoids long-term antithyroid drug risks. Long-term methimazole is less suitable because relapse after stopping is common, and surgery is not needed without a large goiter, compressive symptoms, or eye disease. Iodine-131, 15 mCi, booked for next week. [S]</item><item>Stop methimazole three days before treatment; restarting it afterwards is not needed while free T4 is only mildly raised and he is on a beta-blocker. [S]</item><item>Continue metoprolol until he is no longer thyrotoxic, then taper. [S]</item><item>No steroid cover; report any eye symptoms promptly. [S]</item><item>Free T4 and total T3 within one to two months of treatment, then every four to six weeks until stable. [S]</item><item>Hypothyroidism is expected within two to three months. Start levothyroxine once free T4 falls below normal, at a dose below full replacement because of his age and recent atrial fibrillation, and titrate by TSH every four to eight weeks to 0.4 to 4.0 mIU/L. [S]</item><item>Reassess anticoagulation once he is no longer thyrotoxic and sinus rhythm has been confirmed on ambulatory monitoring. [S]</item><item>Written radiation-safety instructions for the first week after treatment. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Whitford the same day; the patient agrees with radioactive iodine and the follow-up plan. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Endocrinology will give the radioactive iodine next week. Primary care will monitor thyroid levels and levothyroxine dosing on the CMID hypothyroidism schedule. Endocrinology will see him again for eye symptoms, persistent hyperthyroidism at six months, or difficulty reaching the TSH target. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
